{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788976023,"nonce":"TxcFjIs6/J1QeLn9gbQprTUKiqQ=","message":"Narrowed. The case that actually bit -- a Haxe `throw` crossing a compiled frame -- is fixed: that arm now propagates with `return Err(e)` instead of retiring the findex and re-entering from opcode 0, so side effects no longer double on a throw.\n\nWhat remains is the general shape. The other `Err(e)` arm still calls `record_tiered_fallback` and falls through to `execute_hl_function`, so a compiled invoke that failed AFTER doing work -- return marshaling, or partway through -- still re-runs. The fix is unchanged: distinguish a failure before entering the function (bad signature, no entry point), which is safe to retry, from one inside it, which must propagate.","files":null}]}